 "intro": "The last thing the computer needs is exactly 1,024 \u2014 and there is only a 1. A robot can double it: teach one to take the 1 out of its box, set it on Mimi, put the copy in the box, and drop the original on the copy. Wake Ruby to loosen its thought, give it the box and press Run. But a doubling robot never stops on its own \u2014 2, 4, 8\u2026 \u2014 and Stop lets it finish the round it is in. So watch the box and press Stop (or the full stop key) when it says 512. Stopped short? Run and then Stop straight away is one more round. Then give the bird the 1,024.",
 "goal": "exactly 1,024",
 "hints": [
  "Give the robot the box. In its imagination: click the 1 to take it, click Mimi to set it on her platform, click the copy in the tray, and click the empty hole to put the copy in the box.",
  "Now click the original on Mimi\u2019s platform and click the number in the box: a number dropped on a number adds, and 1 on 1 is 2.",
  "Its thought wants exactly a 1. Wake Ruby and click the 1 in its thought until it says \u201cany number\u201d. Then give it the box and press Run.",
  "It doubles every round and it will not stop by itself. Stop lets it finish the round it is in, so press Stop \u2014 or the full stop key \u2014 when the box says 512. If it stops at 512, press Run and then Stop straight away: that is one more round, 1,024. Past it is 2,048, and there is no way back but Start over.",
  "Here\u2019s what I\u2019d do. Drop the box on the robot; take the 1, set it on Mimi, take the copy and drop it in the box, take the original off the platform and drop it on the copy. Leave the thought bubble; wake Ruby and loosen the 1 in its thought. Give it the box, press Run, and press Stop when the number reads 512; if it stops at 512, Run and Stop again for one more round. Take the 1,024 out and give it to the bird."
 ],
